+
+/* Emulated 64-bit atomics for GPUs without any (Apple7, M1).
+ *
+ * Every 64-bit atomic takes a 32-bit spin lock picked by hashing its address
+ * in a device wide table (struct kk_device::atomic64_locks, bound at
+ * buffer(3)); under the lock, the two 32-bit halves are read and written with
+ * 32-bit atomics, between device scope fences. All the operations are exact,
+ * returned values included, as long as every 64-bit atomic on a given address
+ * goes through this path, which it does here.
+ *
+ * The lock loop exits on the result of the release (see emit_locked_rmw): a
+ * SIMD group whose lanes compete for the same lock never waits for the losers
+ * before the winner releases it.
+ *
+ * min/max whose result is unused first read the high word without the lock:
+ * when it alone proves the operation has nothing to do (the stored value is
+ * beyond the operand), that read is a valid linearization point and the lock
+ * is skipped. This is the common case of Nanite's visibility buffer
+ * (depth << 32 | id) for occluded fragments.
+ *
+ * 64-bit image atomics (D3D12 64-bit typed UAVs, R32G32_UINT memory viewed as
+ * R64_UINT) run on the linear memory of the image, see
+ * struct kk_image::atomic64_linear and KK_ATOMIC64_ADDR_BITS. Loads and
+ * stores of those images go through memory too, so they see the atomics of
+ * the same dispatch. An image without that memory falls back to 32-bit
+ * texture reads and writes, and its atomics do nothing.
+ *
+ * Shared memory 64-bit atomics use the same device locks, hashed by offset,
+ * on threadgroup data.
+ */
+
+#include "kk_descriptor_types.h"
